  "full_response": "Designing RESTful APIs involves several best practices to ensure they are efficient, easy to use, and scalable. Below are some key guidelines to follow:\n\n1. **Use HTTP Methods Appropriately:**\n   - **GET**: Retrieve resources.\n   - **POST**: Create new resources.\n   - **PUT**: Update existing resources (including creation if ID is specified).\n   - **PATCH**: Partially update existing resources.\n   - **DELETE**: Remove resources.\n   \n2. **Resource Naming:**\n   - Use plural nouns to represent collections (e.g., `/users`).\n   - Use a consistent naming convention (e.g., `/users/{id}`).\n   - Avoid using verbs in URLs; rely on HTTP methods for actions.\n\n3. **Stateless Interactions:**\n   - Each API request from a client to server must contain all information needed to understand and process the request. The server should not store any client context between requests.\n\n4. **Use Logical Identifiers:**\n   - Use URIs that are meaningful within the domain.\n   - URIs should be human-readable, if possible.\n\n5. **Version Your API:**\n   - Include a version in your API (e.g., `/v1/users`) to manage changes without breaking existing clients.\n\n6. **Leverage HTTP Status Codes:**\n   - Use appropriate status codes (e.g., 200 OK, 404 Not Found, 400 Bad Request, 201 Created, 204 No Content).\n\n7. **Format Data Properly:**\n   - Use JSON or XML for data representation as it's widely supported and easy to parse.\n   - Ensure consistent use of data formats and structures.\n\n8. **Pagination:**\n   - Implement pagination for collections that could return large datasets (e.g., `/users?page=2&size=10`).\n\n9. **Error Handling:**\n   - Provide meaningful error messages with details and corresponding HTTP status codes (e.g., 400 for bad requests, 401 for unauthorized access).\n   - Use an error response structure to make it consistent.\n\n10. **HATEOAS (Hypermedia As The Engine Of Application State):**\n    - Where applicable, include hypermedia links with responses to guide clients on state transitions.\n\n11. **Security:**\n    - Use HTTPS to ensure secure data transmission.\n    - Implement proper authentication and authorization mechanisms (e.g., OAuth2, JWT).\n    - Validate input to protect against SQL injection and other attacks.\n\n12. **Rate Limiting & Throttling:",
